body:
When you drop the braces on an `if`, the compiler has to decide which `if` a trailing
`else` belongs to. C++ resolves it by attaching the `else` to the **nearest unmatched
`if`**, which is often not what the indentation says.

```c++
if (a > 0)
	if (b > 0)
		cout << "both positive";
else
	cout << "a is not positive";   // WRONG: this else belongs to the inner if
```

The indentation claims the `else` pairs with the outer `if`. It does not. It pairs with
`if (b > 0)`, so the message prints when `a > 0` and `b <= 0`, and nothing prints at all
when `a <= 0`.

The fix is braces:

```c++
if (a > 0) {
	if (b > 0)
		cout << "both positive";
} else {
	cout << "a is not positive";
}
```

This is a [[Logic Errors|logic error]], not a [[Compile Errors|compile error]]: the code
is valid C++ and builds cleanly, it just does the wrong thing. Use braces on every
[[Conditional]] with a nested `if` inside it and the problem cannot happen.

The grammar-level version of the same thing shows up in
[[INTRODUCTION TO COMPUTABILITY]]: the dangling else is the standard example of an
**ambiguous** [[Context-Free Grammars|context-free grammar]], one where a single string
has two valid parse trees. Languages resolve it with a disambiguation rule bolted on
outside the grammar, which is exactly the "nearest unmatched `if`" rule above.

